--TEST--
`Core\Response::html` writes the carrier whichever door made one — the lift, the html template, the composition, the escape and the join — `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The claim is asked of every entrance rather than of the one that is easiest
// to write: a member that read the slot of a lifted literal and not of a
// composed one would print plausibly on its own line. `rule:core-classes/html-auto-escape`
// is where the doors are named, and the one thing they have in common is that
// each already decided what may be raw.
Core\Response::html("<b>bold</b>" as Core\Html\Markup);
echo "\n";

Core\Response::html(html`<i>italic</i>`);
echo "\n";

Core\Response::html(("<b>" as Core\Html\Markup) + ("</b>" as Core\Html\Markup));
echo "\n";

Core\Response::html(Core\Html::escape("Tom & Jerry"));
echo "\n";

array<Core\Html\Markup> $rows = [html`<li>one</li>`, html`<li>two</li>`];
Core\Response::html(Core\Html::join($rows, "<br>" as Core\Html\Markup));
echo "\n";

// The empty carrier is the edge on the other side: a `Markup` carrying no
// bytes writes no bytes, rather than a member that treats an empty body as
// nothing to do and skips the whole write.
Core\Response::html("" as Core\Html\Markup);
echo "|", "\n";
--EXPECT--
<b>bold</b>
<i>italic</i>
<b></b>
Tom &amp; Jerry
<li>one</li><br><li>two</li>
|
